    "question": {
      ":question/id": "4adc901d-8305-5972-bd2c-d1f53230ea31",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6x-6 & =-6x-6 \\\\ -6x-6+6x & =-6x-6+6x \\\\ -6 & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-6x-6=-6x-6$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-2 & =2x-2 \\\\ 2x-2-2x & =2x-2-2x \\\\ -2 & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $2x-2=2x-2$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5x & =-4x+9 \\\\ -5x+5x & =-4x+9+5x \\\\ 0 & =x+9 \\\\ 0-9 & =x+9-9 \\\\ x & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $-5x=-4x+9$ has the unique solution $x=-9.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $-6x-6=-6x-6$; $2x-2=2x-2$ only.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "2d5dce52-eadb-5453-af04-dda5a07b7b77",
