    "question": {
      ":question/id": "3df8c321-af01-5b74-9f6e-7e55c0a5fa21",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x+1 & =7x-4 \\\\ 7x+1-7x & =7x-4-7x \\\\ 1 & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7x+4 & =-7x+4 \\\\ -7x+4+7x & =-7x+4+7x \\\\ 4 & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-7x+4=-7x+4$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6x-6 & =-5x-4 \\\\ -6x-6+6x & =-5x-4+6x \\\\ -6 & =x-4 \\\\ -6+4 & =x-4+4 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $-6x-6=-5x-4$ has the unique solution $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $-7x+4=-7x+4$ only.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "7ef24d7b-1dab-511c-91ee-442a22837115",
