  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186110719,
      "id": "ff4e9141-5743-55f1-a8be-3e28cb047eca",
      "title": "Identifying Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "d9ae2f1f-e007-5e65-8742-fc8112f881c4",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-4 & =3x-4 \\\\ 2x-4-2x & =3x-4-2x \\\\ -4 & =x-4 \\\\ -4+4 & =x-4+4 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2x-4=3x-4$ has the unique solution $x=0.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-6 & =5x-6 \\\\ 5x-6-5x & =5x-6-5x \\\\ -6 & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $5x-6=5x-6$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+2 & =6x+2 \\\\ 5x+2-5x & =6x+2-5x \\\\ 2 & =x+2 \\\\ 2-2 & =x+2-2 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $5x+2=6x+2$ has the unique solution $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $5x-6=5x-6$ only.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "8dce0bc3-422d-52b6-a566-c374221f41f8",
