    "question": {
      ":question/id": "38bbe56b-8d3f-5ced-bc61-28f2517490fe",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+4 & =-3x+4 \\\\ -2x+4+3x & =-3x+4+3x \\\\ x+4 & =4 \\\\ x+4-4 & =4-4 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $-2x+4=-3x+4$ has the unique solution $x=0.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2 & =4x-5 \\\\ 3x+2-3x & =4x-5-3x \\\\ 2 & =x-5 \\\\ 2+5 & =x-5+5 \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $3x+2=4x-5$ has the unique solution $x=7.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-3 & =5x-3 \\\\ 5x-3-5x & =5x-3-5x \\\\ -3 & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $5x-3=5x-3$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $5x-3=5x-3$ only.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
