    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e02a984a-cd4e-517d-b42a-c183f2502f87",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x-3 & =-5x-4 \\\\ -4x-3+5x & =-5x-4+5x \\\\ x-3 & =-4 \\\\ x-3+3 & =-4+3 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $-4x-3=-5x-4$ has the unique solution $x=-1.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-7 & =4x-7 \\\\ 4x-7-4x & =4x-7-4x \\\\ -7 & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $4x-7=4x-7$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-5 & =5x-5 \\\\ 5x-5-5x & =5x-5-5x \\\\ -5 & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $5x-5=5x-5$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $4x-7=4x-7$; $5x-5=5x-5$ only.",
