    "question": {
      ":question/id": "4434dacf-534a-5f34-8ffd-c26bfafef991",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+6 & =-3x-8 \\\\ -2x+6+3x & =-3x-8+3x \\\\ x+6 & =-8 \\\\ x+6-6 & =-8-6 \\\\ x & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $-2x+6=-3x-8$ has the unique solution $x=-14.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-1 & =7x-1 \\\\ 7x-1-7x & =7x-1-7x \\\\ -1 & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $7x-1=7x-1$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7x & =-7x \\\\ -7x+7x & =-7x+7x \\\\ 0 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-7x=-7x$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $7x-1=7x-1$; $-7x=-7x$ only.",
