    "question": {
      ":question/id": "5809ccd4-6636-5274-a220-103e2f352575",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+8 & =-x+8 \\\\ -x+8+x & =-x+8+x \\\\ 8 & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-x+8=-x+8$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7x-5 & =-7x-5 \\\\ -7x-5+7x & =-7x-5+7x \\\\ -5 & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-7x-5=-7x-5$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-8 & =2x-8 \\\\ 3x-8-2x & =2x-8-2x \\\\ x-8 & =-8 \\\\ x-8+8 & =-8+8 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $3x-8=2x-8$ has the unique solution $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $-x+8=-x+8$; $-7x-5=-7x-5$ only.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "f2a2dfa5-d1dc-5e36-8384-55ea1cb6aae1",
