  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186110719,
      "id": "ff4e9141-5743-55f1-a8be-3e28cb047eca",
      "title": "Identifying Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "122e6709-c921-5ac7-b785-af99b85fc419",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-1 & =5x-1 \\\\ 5x-1-5x & =5x-1-5x \\\\ -1 & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $5x-1=5x-1$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x-3 & =-3x-3 \\\\ -3x-3+3x & =-3x-3+3x \\\\ -3 & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-3x-3=-3x-3$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6x-7 & =-6x-7 \\\\ -6x-7+6x & =-6x-7+6x \\\\ -7 & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-6x-7=-6x-7$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $5x-1=5x-1$; $-3x-3=-3x-3$; $-6x-7=-6x-7$ only.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "4738a8d2-4d4d-5d70-b150-6524420e8b31",
