    "question": {
      ":question/id": "10a10bdc-cee0-50de-b8ce-a8e93d4a3813",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x-5 & =-4x-5 \\\\ -4x-5+4x & =-4x-5+4x \\\\ -5 & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-4x-5=-4x-5$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+4 & =3x+4 \\\\ 2x+4-2x & =3x+4-2x \\\\ 4 & =x+4 \\\\ 4-4 & =x+4-4 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2x+4=3x+4$ has the unique solution $x=0.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x-5 & =-3x+4 \\\\ -4x-5+4x & =-3x+4+4x \\\\ -5 & =x+4 \\\\ -5-4 & =x+4-4 \\\\ x & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $-4x-5=-3x+4$ has the unique solution $x=-9.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $-4x-5=-4x-5$ only.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "59766fd5-c093-5a6b-83eb-2510b84850e9",
