      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6x+7 & =-6x+7 \\\\ -6x+7+6x & =-6x+7+6x \\\\ 7 & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-6x+7=-6x+7$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+3 & =-2x+5 \\\\ -2x+3+2x & =-2x+5+2x \\\\ 3 & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7x-8 & =-7x-5 \\\\ -7x-8+7x & =-7x-5+7x \\\\ -8 & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $-6x+7=-6x+7$ only.",
