  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186110719,
      "id": "ff4e9141-5743-55f1-a8be-3e28cb047eca",
      "title": "Identifying Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "7a55632a-c2bf-5025-97d3-a11ab7025252",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x+4 & =-4x+4 \\\\ -4x+4+4x & =-4x+4+4x \\\\ 4 & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-4x+4=-4x+4$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+6 & =3x+6 \\\\ 3x+6-3x & =3x+6-3x \\\\ 6 & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $3x+6=3x+6$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5x+3 & =-5x \\\\ -5x+3+5x & =-5x+5x \\\\ 3 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $-4x+4=-4x+4$; $3x+6=3x+6$ only.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "4410bb23-f4b5-5bd3-84d3-439ea009a57d",
