    "question": {
      ":question/id": "8fb97857-9417-5282-b99b-8859a7e0ae6c",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x-2 & =-3x-2 \\\\ -3x-2+3x & =-3x-2+3x \\\\ -2 & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-3x-2=-3x-2$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7x-8 & =-6x-5 \\\\ -7x-8+7x & =-6x-5+7x \\\\ -8 & =x-5 \\\\ -8+5 & =x-5+5 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $-7x-8=-6x-5$ has the unique solution $x=-3.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+1 & =4x-8 \\\\ 3x+1-3x & =4x-8-3x \\\\ 1 & =x-8 \\\\ 1+8 & =x-8+8 \\\\ x & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $3x+1=4x-8$ has the unique solution $x=9.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $-3x-2=-3x-2$ only.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "d60ee307-630e-54b6-ae9b-fdc5166e10e7",
