      ":question/id": "31195734-9d15-55c3-b664-30aa80fa8371",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+8 & =6x+7 \\\\ 5x+8-5x & =6x+7-5x \\\\ 8 & =x+7 \\\\ 8-7 & =x+7-7 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $5x+8=6x+7$ has the unique solution $x=1.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5x+7 & =-5x+7 \\\\ -5x+7+5x & =-5x+7+5x \\\\ 7 & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-5x+7=-5x+7$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+7 & =-2x+6 \\\\ -2x+7+2x & =-2x+6+2x \\\\ 7 & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $-5x+7=-5x+7$ only.",
