      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-2 & =7x-2 \\\\ 7x-2-7x & =7x-2-7x \\\\ -2 & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $7x-2=7x-2$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7x+3 & =-6x+3 \\\\ -7x+3+7x & =-6x+3+7x \\\\ 3 & =x+3 \\\\ 3-3 & =x+3-3 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $-7x+3=-6x+3$ has the unique solution $x=0.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x-7 & =-2x-7 \\\\ -2x-7+2x & =-2x-7+2x \\\\ -7 & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-2x-7=-2x-7$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $7x-2=7x-2$; $-2x-7=-2x-7$ only.",
