    "question": {
      ":question/id": "a66ba3c1-e83b-5bfa-946e-8cf56f74406d",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+1 & =5x+1 \\\\ 5x+1-5x & =5x+1-5x \\\\ 1 & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $5x+1=5x+1$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5x & =-4x \\\\ -5x+5x & =-4x+5x \\\\ 0 & =x \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $-5x=-4x$ has the unique solution $x=0.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x-1 & =-x-1 \\\\ -x-1+x & =-x-1+x \\\\ -1 & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-x-1=-x-1$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $5x+1=5x+1$; $-x-1=-x-1$ only.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "c7ed1a08-59a0-5095-8112-de90baa9a2b5",
          ":answer-field/key": "$5x+1=5x+1$",
