  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186110719,
      "id": "ff4e9141-5743-55f1-a8be-3e28cb047eca",
      "title": "Identifying Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e968248b-e137-5c53-a06e-a5403cc7273a",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7x & =-7x \\\\ -7x+7x & =-7x+7x \\\\ 0 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-7x=-7x$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6x-9 & =-5x-9 \\\\ -6x-9+6x & =-5x-9+6x \\\\ -9 & =x-9 \\\\ -9+9 & =x-9+9 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $-6x-9=-5x-9$ has the unique solution $x=0.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-4 & =2x-4 \\\\ 2x-4-2x & =2x-4-2x \\\\ -4 & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $2x-4=2x-4$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $-7x=-7x$; $2x-4=2x-4$ only.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "88c44175-c8e2-5631-9a2e-14cf17e6bc3b",
