    "question": {
      ":question/id": "ab7f6a5e-542d-56b4-a513-93033649dd8c",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+7 & =x+7 \\\\ x+7-x & =x+7-x \\\\ 7 & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $x+7=x+7$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5x+5 & =-6x+5 \\\\ -5x+5+6x & =-6x+5+6x \\\\ x+5 & =5 \\\\ x+5-5 & =5-5 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $-5x+5=-6x+5$ has the unique solution $x=0.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =3x+4 \\\\ 3x-3x & =3x+4-3x \\\\ 0 & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x+7=x+7$ only.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "c413726c-6e77-5703-91ee-32fa9a56f044",
